NAME
       usermod - change an existing user account

SYNOPSIS
       usermod [OPTIONS] LOGIN

DESCRIPTION
       usermod changes the settings of the account LOGIN in /etc/passwd,
       /etc/shadow and /etc/group: its groups, shell, home directory, name,
       UID, expiry date, or whether it is locked.

       The most common use is adding a user to a group. Be careful: -G alone
       REPLACES the list of supplementary groups, so the user leaves every
       group you did not name. Add -a (append) to keep the existing ones:
       usermod -aG wheel student.

       Group changes take effect at the user's next login; a running session
       keeps its old groups (check with id USER, which reads the files).

OPTIONS
       -a, --append
              With -G: add the groups, keep the existing ones.

       -G, --groups GROUP1[,GROUP2...]
              Set the supplementary groups (comma separated, no spaces).

       -g, --gid GROUP
              Change the primary group.

       -s, --shell SHELL
              Change the login shell, for example to /sbin/nologin.

       -c, --comment TEXT
              Change the comment (full name) field.

       -d, --home DIR
              Change the home directory. Add -m to move the old one's
              contents there.

       -m, --move-home
              With -d: move the home directory's contents.

       -l, --login NEWNAME
              Rename the account (the home directory keeps its name unless you
              also use -d and -m).

       -u, --uid UID
              Change the UID. Files in the home directory are given the new
              number; files elsewhere must be fixed by hand.

       -L, --lock
              Lock the password (puts a ! in front of it in /etc/shadow).

       -U, --unlock
              Unlock the password.

       -e, --expiredate YYYY-MM-DD
              The date the account stops working. -e '' removes the date;
              -e 1 (or a past date) disables the account at once.

       -f, --inactive DAYS
              Days after the password expires before the account is disabled.

       -p, --password HASH
              Set an already encrypted password.

       -o, --non-unique
              With -u, allow a UID another account already has.

EXAMPLES
       Give student full sudo rights by adding him to wheel:

           usermod -aG wheel student
           id student

       Change a user's shell so he can no longer log in:

           usermod -s /sbin/nologin harry

       Make an account expire at the end of the year:

           usermod -e 2026-12-31 natasha

       Lock and later unlock an account:

           usermod -L harry
           usermod -U harry

